WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.860
Good morning Hank it's tuesday

00:00:00.860 --> 00:00:02.800
So I saw the Star Wars movie again this weekend

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.560
and on my fourth viewing I noticed something really troubling.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:07.900
By the way this video contains no significant spoilers.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.280
Unless you go to the movies primarily to see people separated from their limbs.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.520
But even then, very mild spoilers.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:18.260
Lots of people have complained that the Force Awakens is just a re-hash of the original Star Wars movie, right?

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:20.700
Even the creator of Star Wars, George Lucas said:

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:23.340
"They wanted to do a retro movie. I don't like that."

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.340
Now I think that view of the movie over-simplifies it,

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:29.440
but here's the thing, if it's such a re-hash, where are all the severed hands?!

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.300
I severed my own hand by accident over here, but I still got it! Don't worry.

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:34.620
So Hank, as you pointed out in a video more than five years ago

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:39.100
the first six Star Wars movies contain no fewer than thirteen severed hands.

00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:44.680
Like, Anakin Skywalker, despite having two total hands, manages to get one of his hands cut off three separate times.

00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:49.800
There's even a phrase in the Star Wars universe for using your lightsaber to amputate an opponent's hand:

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:50.560
Cho Mai.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.420
So Hank, thirty-seven hundred fifty years ago,

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:55.180
the Babylonian king, Hammurabi, developed a legal code,

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:59.540
that famously uses an "eye for an eye" approach to legal retaliation.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.780
And the Star Wars universe is kind of similar, except it's a hand for a hand.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.980
Like, it's no coincidence that over the course of the movies Anakin Skywalker also chops off three hands.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:12.940
In Star Wars, the number one way to risk amputation of your own hand is to amputate someone else's hand.

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:17.120
Well, unless you're Obi-Wan Kenobi, who by my count removes nine separate limbs from people

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.200
while somehow managing to remain a tetrapod.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.700
Which, yes, is the word for animals with four limbs.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:25.480
So anyway, when George Lucas has been asked about this, he always just says:

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.360
"Well that's what happens when you play with swords."

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.340
Which, you know, fair enough. But there's plenty of swordplay in The Force Awakens and yet

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:36.240
every single hand remains attached to its body for the entire movie!

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.620
It's unconscionable!

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.780
Meanwhile, Marvel movies have begun chopping off hands as an homage to Star Wars

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.720
and no, I am not making that up.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:48.680
In fact, Marvel Studios president, Kevin Feige has said that it's because of Star Wars that all the so called

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.460
"Phase Two" Marvel movies feature an arm amputation.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.020
Captain America: Winter Soldier?

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:53.900
Definitely.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:54.960
Guardians of the Galaxy?

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:55.460
Check!

00:01:55.460 --> 00:01:56.200
Iron Man 3?

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.960
Yes! And then they burn it.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:01:59.260
And Ant-Man? Kinda.

00:01:59.260 --> 00:02:02.680
Insofar as somebody loses an arm just before losing the rest of themselves.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.740
Nobody's harshing on those movies for their nostalgia.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.640
Hank, as previously noted, I really liked The Force Awakens.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:10.980
It is, at times, nostalgic, but I don't think nostalgia is all bad.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.940
In fact, I'd argue the original Star Wars was also nostalgic.

00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:16.000
It harkened back to American Westerns

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:21.240
and to some non-existent bygone era when good was really good and evil was really evil.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.160
Really, if it hadn't been for the inexcusable failure to chop off any hands,

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.080
The Force Awakens would have been a *great* movie.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.160
So Hank, I think some of the blowback is just do the movie's commercial success.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.260
Most people are never comfortable loving something unironically, especially when it becomes quite popular.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:38.320
But I think George Lucas's feelings about The Force Awakens are probably about something deeper

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.500
and not directly related to it's lack of severed hands.

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.000
Basically, I think it's really hard for creators, even very successful ones,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:49.800
to see their work go out into the world and be loved for reasons they didn't intend.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.960
Like I've found that one of the pleasures and terrors of writing for an audience

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.060
is that the book someone reads is never quite the book you wrote.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:01.180
Inevitably bring their own experiences and expectations and desires to a story

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.280
and in that process, they change it.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.100
To use an extreme example, when I was in high school I read The Great Gatsby, and I thought it was crap.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.640
That wasn't The Great Gatsby's fault.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:09.660
On the other hand Hank,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:13.560
when we were little kids we watched the movie
Grease 2 over and over and over again on VHS

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.500
And by forging that mediocre movie in the smithy of our souls, we were somehow able to make it great.

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.080
Now of course that isn't to minimize the responsibility of creators to their creations.

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:26.300
It's just to say that once you've done your best and something is out in the world, you have to let it go.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:28.860
Basically Hank, to me, making stuff is like a Wookie.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.440
If you try to hold onto the stuff you make with too tight a grip,

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.080
it'll rip your arms right off.

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:34.797
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

